\description{
A mean/sigma link is made in three steps, and each step is a function, so that a link can be
followed one step at a time, or made from points prepared elsewhere:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{link <- points |>
  kits_link_screen() |>          # 1 one round of screening
  kits_link_line() |>            # 2 the mean/sigma line
  kits_link_error(n_boot = 200)  # 3 the linking error
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{\link[=kits_linking]{kits_linking()}} and \code{\link[=kits_linking_d]{kits_linking_d()}} run the same three steps on the thresholds of the common
items or of the anchors. The points are a data.frame with one row per point: \code{id} (its name, for
example \code{"lana01:2"}), \code{item} (the item it belongs to, for the resampling), \code{from} (the value on
the scale to be converted) and \code{to} (the value on the target scale).
}
\details{
\enumerate{
\item \code{kits_link_screen()}: fits the line through all points (\code{intercept0}, \code{slope0}), computes
each residual e = to - (intercept0 + slope0 x from), and drops the points with |e| beyond
\code{cut} x SD(e).
The screening is done once, not repeated.
When the residuals are almost all 0 (the points lie exactly on a line), no point is dropped.
\item \code{kits_link_line()}: the mean/sigma line through the kept points, to = intercept + slope x
from, with slope = SD(to) / SD(from) (the ratio of the two SDs) and intercept = mean(to) -
slope x mean(from) (it aligns the two means). Kolen and Brennan (2014) call the slope A and
the intercept B. The result is a link without its linking error.
\item \code{kits_link_error()}: resamples whole items \code{n_boot} times and redoes steps 1 and 2 each time;
the SD of the intercept and of the slope over the resamples is the linking error
(\code{intercept_error}, \code{slope_error}).
When an item has several points (each step of a polytomous item), the points are not
independent, so the whole item is resampled together (\code{item}).
Resamples that cannot be computed (for example, the same item drawn every time) are left out
and counted in \code{n_failed}. \code{n_boot = 0} removes the linking error.
}

Two things to watch:
\itemize{
\item With few points, a deviating point inflates the residual SD, so it may not be dropped itself.
For example, with 10 anchor items and one off by 12 D, that item is not dropped; with 15 items
it is. So also look at the residuals in \code{points}.
\item When the points of one item shift together, often the slope is pulled away, instead of that
item being dropped.
}

The three steps give the same link as \code{kits_mean_sigma(from, to, id, item, cut, n_boot, seed)}
of kitsPreview 0.20.0 (removed in 0.23.0). No other package is used: the line is closed-form base R,
so \code{\link[=kits_code]{kits_code()}} writes it out in full. kits has the same three functions, with more screening
rules (\code{spread}, \code{unit}) and a first step \code{kits_link_points()}; here the screening is the rule of
kits' default (\code{spread = "sd"}, \code{unit = "step"}).
}
